        <p>Hersham is a neighbouring village rather than a seventh area of Walton: see <a href="/hersham/living/">living in Hersham</a>.</p>

        <h3 id="hersham">Hersham</h3>
        <p>Hersham is a village in its own right, not a district of Walton. It has its own centre around the green, its own shops and surgery, and its own railway station on a different line, and it keeps a separate identity that long predates the shared KT12 postal address (Walton-on-Thames.org, 2026b).</p>
        <p>For anyone weighing up the two, the practical differences are the ones that matter: Hersham station is a smaller station with a less frequent service than Walton-on-Thames, the village centre is compact rather than a full town centre, and schools, clubs and everyday services are covered in the dedicated Hersham guide rather than repeated here.</p>
        <p class="deeper-link"><a href="/hersham/living/">Living in Hersham: schools, doctors, getting around and council services</a> · <a href="/hersham/">the Hersham guide</a> · <a href="/hersham-railway-station/">Hersham railway station</a></p>

        <h3 id="whiteley-village">Whiteley Village</h3>
        <p>Whiteley Village, south of Hersham, is not conventional housing and cannot be bought or rented on the open market. It is a charitable almshouse village run by the Whiteley Homes Trust, which provides 264 one and two-bedroom almshouse cottages for independent living, alongside Huntley House, 51 self-contained extra-care flats (Whiteley Homes Trust, n.d.b).</p>
        <p>Because it is an almshouse charity, residents have to qualify. The Trust asks applicants to be 65 or over if male and 60 or over if female, with a net annual income of no more than £25,000 for a single person or £40,000 for a couple, and capital of no more than £30,000 per person or £50,000 per couple. Applicants must have lived in the UK for five or more consecutive years and must show a housing need, such as being at risk of involuntary homelessness, a medical or welfare need made worse by their present home, or insecurity in a private shorthold tenancy. The Trust states that it does not currently accept applicants with a dementia diagnosis (Whiteley Homes Trust, n.d.c).</p>
        <p>Applications are made on the Trust's own forms rather than through an agent or the council, so anyone considering it should start at the Trust's application page and check the current criteria and vacancies there (Whiteley Homes Trust, n.d.c).</p>
        <p>The village is a private residential community rather than an attraction, and not everything in it is open to the public. The Whiteley Community Café in the Clubhouse on Octagon Road is open to residents and visitors alike, but the grounds are people's homes.</p>
        <p class="deeper-link"><a href="/hersham/whiteley-village/">Whiteley Village: its history, architecture and how to visit respectfully</a> · <a href="https://www.thewhiteleyhomestrust.org.uk/living-at-whiteley/apply-to-live-at-whiteley" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Apply to live at Whiteley, Whiteley Homes Trust ↗</a></p>


        <h3 id="geography">What counts as Walton-on-Thames?</h3>
        <p>This is more complicated than the postal address suggests.</p>
        <p>The Office for National Statistics defines a <strong>Walton-on-Thames built-up area</strong> as a statistical geography. Census 2021 data can therefore be reported for that geography, but it is not the same thing as the KT12 postcode district, current council wards or the historic parish (Office for National Statistics, 2023).</p>
        <p>The <strong>KT12 postcode district is broader than Walton-on-Thames</strong>. It includes Hersham and Whiteley Village addresses: Whiteley Homes Trust gives its own address as Octagon Road, Walton on Thames, KT12 4EH, and sold-price records for streets in Hersham such as Southdown Road and Queens Road carry KT12 postcodes with Walton-on-Thames as the postal town (Whiteley Homes Trust, n.d.a; Rightmove, 2026). Hersham is a distinct village with its own centre and identity, while Whiteley Village is a distinct residential community south of Hersham. Both are covered by this site because of their close practical links with the town, but neither should simply be counted as a Walton neighbourhood.</p>
        <p>Walton-on-Thames railway station adds another complication. It serves Walton and north Hersham, and sits much closer to some residential streets than it does to Walton town centre (Walton-on-Thames.org, 2026a).</p>
        <p>For anyone comparing properties, the most useful unit is therefore the <strong>actual street and immediate neighbourhood</strong>, not the postal town or the postcode.</p>

        <h2 id="housing">Housing and the real cost of living</h2>
        <p>Walton sits within an expensive borough housing market, but no single headline figure accurately represents every part of the town.</p>

        <h3>Official Elmbridge market context</h3>
        <p>The Office for National Statistics, using UK House Price Index data from HM Land Registry, reported an <strong>average Elmbridge house price of £744,000 in June 2026</strong>, down 2.1% from June 2025. The figure was provisional when published (Office for National Statistics, 2026).</p>
        <p>In the same release, the average price paid by first-time buyers in Elmbridge was <strong>£500,000</strong>, while home movers paid an average of <strong>£948,000</strong> (Office for National Statistics, 2026).</p>
        <p>Those figures are useful context, but they cover the whole borough. Elmbridge includes Walton, Weybridge, Esher, Cobham, the Moleseys, the Dittons and other settlements with different property markets.</p>

        <h3>Why this guide does not quote a "Walton average" from a property portal</h3>
        <p>Commercial portals publish useful sold-price data, but their geography does not always match the town itself.</p>
        <p>Current Rightmove results labelled <strong>Walton-on-Thames</strong> include properties in Hersham, because Hersham commonly uses Walton-on-Thames as its postal town. Sold entries for Southdown Road, Vaux Crescent and Queens Road, all in Hersham, appear under the Walton-on-Thames heading. A single portal average can therefore mix more than one local market (Rightmove, 2026).</p>
        <p>For that reason, this guide does not present a portal average as an official Walton-only figure. For a purchase, the most useful evidence is <strong>recent completed sales of genuinely comparable homes on the same street or in the same immediate area</strong>.</p>
        <p>A future property page on this site should calculate a reproducible Walton-only measure directly from HM Land Registry Price Paid Data, using a clearly documented geographic definition.</p>

        <h3>Renting</h3>
        <p>Official rental statistics are also published at Elmbridge level rather than for Walton. The average private rent across Elmbridge was <strong>£1,867 a month in July 2026</strong>, up 1.1% year on year (Office for National Statistics, 2026).</p>
        <p>Average monthly rents by bedroom number were (Office for National Statistics, 2026):</p>
        <ul>
          <li>one bedroom: <strong>£1,247</strong></li>
          <li>two bedrooms: <strong>£1,569</strong></li>
          <li>three bedrooms: <strong>£1,912</strong></li>
          <li>four or more bedrooms: <strong>£2,848</strong></li>
        </ul>
        <p>These are borough-wide averages, not expected rents for an individual Walton property.</p>

        <h3 id="costs">Council Tax</h3>
        <p>For the 2026/27 financial year, total Council Tax for an <strong>Elmbridge Band D property outside Claygate Parish is £2,556.87</strong> (Elmbridge Borough Council, 2026a).</p>
        <p>That is the total charge published by Elmbridge Borough Council for the borough outside Claygate Parish. The amount due depends on the individual property's valuation band, and properties within Claygate Parish are charged separately (Elmbridge Borough Council, 2026a).</p>
        <p>For commuters, rail fares and parking can also be significant household costs. Anyone moving specifically for the London connection should price the journey they will actually make, including office frequency, ticket type, station access and onward travel in London.</p>

        <h3 id="monthly-costs">What a monthly budget has to cover</h3>
        <p>Beyond the mortgage or rent, a realistic monthly figure for this area needs to allow for Council Tax at the property's own band, utilities and broadband, buildings and contents insurance, the full commute including any station parking and onward travel in London, childcare if relevant, and car ownership where a household keeps one. A flat or a house on an estate may also carry a service charge or other lease obligations, which are property-specific and need checking before an offer.</p>
        <p>This guide does not publish an example household budget, because the numbers that would make it useful, the exact band, tariff, ticket, nursery and lease, are all property-specific. The checks in <a href="#property-checks">before choosing a property</a> are the ones that turn those into real figures.</p>


        <h2 id="transport">Getting around and commuting</h2>
        <p><strong>Direct trains run from Walton-on-Thames to London Waterloo. The fastest scheduled services are usually around 22 to 25 minutes, while many stopping services take around 30 to 35 minutes.</strong> The pattern varies by time of day, at weekends and during engineering work (South Western Railway, 2026; Walton-on-Thames.org, 2026a).</p>
        <p>The headline rail time is only part of the commute. The station is roughly a <strong>20 to 25-minute walk from Walton town centre</strong>, and total journey time varies substantially depending on the street you live on and where in London you need to reach (Walton-on-Thames.org, 2026a).</p>
        <p>The station also has direct services towards Weybridge and Woking, with onward connections across Surrey and the wider south-west rail network (National Rail, 2026).</p>

